summary = (
 'Keep the whirling-fruit system as the main Track 4 build, and run the ballasted plate glider alongside it. '
 'The fruit gives a pre-build number and public data, but it is a vertical sink with no forward glide, so the accepted glide-range law (x <= h*(L/D)max) gives a bound of 0 m and cannot test it. '
 'The plate glider (L/D 3.77 recomputed, paper 3.8) engages that law and the ground-effect and wind-glide laws, and needs only a long channel. '
 'Six alternatives are laid out in the table (plate, printed samara with LEV model, flying-fish fin models, humidity-folding glider, Draco lizard data, flying snake) plus two same-variable neighbour studies. '
 'The strongest rival explanation is the leading-edge-vortex lift account (Lentink 2009; Jung and Rezgui 2023, 2026), which differs from the chosen model only in how lift is closed (explicit LEV lift forms vs a fitted curve); a third account (separated vortex ring, Liu 2025) is for a different seed. '
 'Cheapest extra arm: fit a normal-force LEV model to the public fruit data; whether its code is public was not checked. '
 'Law set 1.8.1 differs from 1.6.0 by six non-flight laws; the flight laws are unchanged and the four flight gaps remain proposed.')

lay = ('The main experiment drops small 3D-printed seed-pods, shaped like maple helicopters, through water and asks which wing curve makes them fall slowest. '
 'These pods only spin and sink, so they never glide sideways, and our rule about how far a glider can travel has nothing to say about them. '
 'A flat plate with a weight at its front is a cheap second experiment: slide the weight and the plate flutters, glides or dives, like a paper plane. '
 'Other options are fish-fin models, a lizard that really glides in the wild, and a fold-up seed glider that bends with damp air. '
 'Scientists disagree on why spinning seeds fall slowly: one account says a small tornado of air stuck to the wing lifts it, the other fits a simple curve to measurements. '
 'We recommend keeping the seed-pod test and adding the plate test next to it.')

gloss = [
 {'term': 'autorotation', 'plain': 'a seed or blade spinning freely as it falls, so its wings generate lift.'},
 {'term': 'blade-element model', 'plain': 'cutting a wing into thin slices, working out the air force on each slice and adding them up.'},
 {'term': 'leading-edge vortex (LEV)', 'plain': 'a small spinning tube of air that stays attached along the front edge of a wing and adds lift.'},
 {'term': 'Polhamus / normal-force model', 'plain': 'two simple formulas for the extra lift a leading-edge vortex gives.'},
 {'term': 'glide ratio (L/D)', 'plain': 'how far a glider travels sideways for each unit it drops.'},
 {'term': 'Reynolds number', 'plain': 'a single number comparing push of the flow to stickiness of the fluid; equal numbers mean a model behaves like the real thing.'},
 {'term': 'quasi-steady', 'plain': 'treating the air force at each moment as if the motion were frozen.'},
 {'term': 'fold angle', 'plain': 'how strongly a wing is curved along its length.'},
 {'term': 'centre of mass', 'plain': 'the balance point of an object.'},
 {'term': 'CFD', 'plain': 'computer simulation of air or water flow.'},
 {'term': 'disk loading', 'plain': 'weight divided by the circle swept by spinning wings.'},
]

